WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.480
NAD+ is an essential cofactor
for hundreds of enzymes

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:08.040
including the purported anti-
aging activities of sirtuins.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.600
In this 10-part video series,

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:16.120
I will go through the alphabet soup of
NAD-boosting supplements on the market:

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:22.360
NR, NMN, NA, NAM, NAD, NADH,
NMNH, NRH, and Tryptophan.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:26.040
I will then cover the
pros and cons of each

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:30.901
and discuss three ways to naturally boost
NAD levels without supplements.

00:00:30.901 --> 00:00:37.808
"Do NAD+ Levels Decline with Age?"

00:00:38.197 --> 00:00:43.280
Our understanding of nicotinamide
adenine dinucleotide (NAD+)

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:48.400
arose from humble beginnings as a factor
noted to enhance yeast fermentation

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:54.960
in a 1906 paper unassumingly entitled
“The Alcoholic Ferment of Yeast-Juice.”

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:58.280
Little did they know that
waves of NAD-related discoveries

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:01.720
would go on to yield a
total of four Nobel Prizes.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:06.320
NAD+ is now known as an essential
molecule for all living organisms,

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:09.240
required for the function
of about 500 enzymes,

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:13.760
including, notably, the extraction
of metabolic energy from food.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:18.120
The 21st century has produced yet
another scientific renaissance for NAD+

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:21.800
with the realization that it was
critical for the activity of sirtuins,

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:27.680
the “guardians of mammalian healthspan”
I detail in my book, How Not to Age.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:31.680
NAD+ is one of the most abundant
molecules in our body.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.360
Once considered relatively stable,

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:38.440
it’s now known to be in a constant state
of synthesis, recycling, and breakdown.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:40.920
The entire pool of NAD+
in some of our tissues

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:44.440
is turned over
several times a day.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:47.720
To maintain cellular vitality
in the face of this turnover,

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:50.760
an adequate supply
of NAD+ precursors

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:55.680
and sufficiently high enzyme activity
synthesizing NAD+ are critical.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:00.080
The importance of NAD+ is exemplified
by the devastating consequences

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:04.480
of a deficiency of NAD+ precursors
like niacin (vitamin B3).

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:06.880
The deficiency syndrome,
called pellagra,

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:09.560
is characterized by the 4 D’s:

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:14.760
dermatitis, dementia, diarrhea,
and, eventually, death.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:18.040
Thankfully, since life as we know
it couldn’t exist without it,

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:21.680
NAD+ and its precursors are
found in everything we eat—

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:24.120
plant, animal, or fungi—

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:25.760
but we need to know
how to get at them.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:29.360
The niacin in corn, for instance,
is tightly bound up,

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:33.440
but it can be released by pre-
soaking in alkaline lime-water.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:35.720
Maize was exported
from Latin America

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:39.040
to become a dietary staple
without the requisite knowledge

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:41.480
about traditional processing
techniques, though,

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:44.320
and an epidemic
of pellagra ensued.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:48.080
An estimated 100,000 Americans
died from pellagra

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:50.280
in the first few decades
of the 20th century

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:54.720
before bread started to be
fortified with niacin in 1938.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:03:00.200
The pitch for NAD+ boosting as an
anti-aging strategy goes as follows:

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:02.400
all species,
including humans,

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.520
naturally experience a decline
in NAD+ levels over time,

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:06.800
and this decline is, in fact,

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:09.600
one of the major
reasons organisms age.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:12.360
By restoring youthful levels,
the argument goes,

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:16.560
these age-related disorders can
be delayed or even reversed.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:19.640
Two leaders in the field,
one from Harvard and the other MIT,

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:22.440
have said, respectively,
that NAD+ boosters may

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:25.440
“hold the promise of increasing
the body’s resilience,

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:27.920
not just to one disease,
but to many,

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:31.360
thereby extending healthy
human lifespan,”

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:34.800
and that sirtuin activation
by NAD+ repletion

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:39.520
“may be the most actionable item
to emerge from aging research.”

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:41.800
Of course, they have
both been involved

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:46.120
in multimillion-dollar dietary
supplement companies.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:50.200
The first premise, that NAD+
levels decline with age,

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:51.760
has been called into question.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:54.320
For example,
this 2022 review

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:59.720
“Age-Dependent Decline of NAD+—
Universal Truth or Confounded Consensus?”

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:05.600
concluded that, despite
systemic claims to the contrary,

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:08.640
the evidence supporting
the premise is very limited.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:11.360
Indeed, the most
comprehensive study to date

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:13.760
found significant changes
in NAD+ levels

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:18.240
in less than half of the tested
tissues in old versus young mice.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:21.840
The human data are
similarly inconsistent.

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:25.640
NAD+ boosting supplement
shills make claims like

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:27.360
“By middle age,
our NAD+ levels

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:30.040
have plummeted to half
that of our youth,”

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:36.000
but the cited source only shows a
drop (in brain levels) of about 13%

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:38.680
between about
the ages 20 to 60.

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:44.040
A similar study estimated about
an 18% drop from age 25 to 70,

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:49.080
both broadly consistent with a
14% drop in spinal tap fluid samples

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:53.080
taken from those over
age 45 (average age 71),

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:56.720
compared to those under
age 45 (average age 34).

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:01.040
It’s unclear if such modest differences
would have any consequences,

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:05.240
and a more recent study found
no significant differences at all

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:09.160
in brain nor muscle levels between
a young group (average age 21)

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:12.880
and an older group
(average age 69).

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:17.240
A study of skin samples found a greater
than 50% drop in young adults

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:19.360
compared to the skin of newborns,

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:23.400
and a further drop of about 60%
from young adults to middle age.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:27.760
However, there did not seem to be
a further decline from middle to old age.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:31.040
There was a small study that found
the NAD+ levels in the liver samples

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:34.280
of six older individuals
(average age 66)

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:39.640
was about 30% lower than that of
six younger individuals (average age 39).

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:43.080
NAD+ levels also may
be lower in macrophage

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:46.000
white blood cells
in older individuals,

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:48.320
but in the blood more generally,

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:50.440
half the studies showed
a decline with age,

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:52.080
and the other half didn’t.

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:54.000
By far the largest study

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:58.080
(enrolling 10 times more people
than the other studies combined)

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:01.080
found a slight drop in NAD+
in the aging bloodstreams

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:04.120
of men but no drop in women.

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:05.600
The bottom line is that,

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:10.720
given the conflicting results from the
remarkably few studies on the subject,

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:15.960
it’s misleading to say NAD+
universally decreases with age.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:18.960
Regardless, the proof
is in the pudding.

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:22.400
What about the second premise
that boosting levels late in life

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:24.560
can improve health and longevity?

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:29.560
We’ll address that question, next.

